{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-19ad5426-3268-4274-83f4-438543e4d044","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-949a8a11bf76ed19","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-ejx7_xl5/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n\nOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n\n## Triage Outcome\n\nNot triaged as below-threshold or docs-only. The strategy is S-sized and touches one\ncomponent (rhods-operator) and one implementing team, but two escape conditions apply:\n\n1. **Multiple distinct work streams** — the scope mixes mechanical code conversion across\n   two binaries with substantive content authoring (a new contribution guideline topic and\n   a published convention specification for external operator adoption, HLR-8). Per the\n   boundary rules this is a work-product type split, not a priority split.\n2. Code work itself spans two architecturally distinct binaries (manager and cloudmanager),\n   each warranting its own PR review cycle.\n\n## Step 1.5 — Staff Engineer Input Handling\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. It is already\nincorporated verbatim into the strategy's Technical Approach (the strategy was regenerated\nagainst it), so no divergences required override logging. The decomposition relies on these\nSME-confirmed facts:\n\n- Exactly 15 action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) —\n  the earlier \"~16 component action files\" figure is wrong and is not used.\n- The module controller tier (module controller + 8 module handlers + 3 module action\n  files) is in scope; the AI-generated architecture description that omitted it is superseded.\n- Three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys\n  (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), missing `resourceKind`.\n- camelCase field naming (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) per controller-runtime practice.\n- cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler and already receives `name`/`namespace` from\n  controller-runtime's request logger; it needs `resourceKind` plus embedded-identity conversion.\n- The former \"Open Question 5\" (component action file count) is answered (8/8) and is not\n  treated as an unknown.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Structured resource context in manager binary reconciliation error logs | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Structured resource context in cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Structured log field convention: contribution guideline and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 manager binary log conversion]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 cloudmanager binary log conversion]\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 convention guideline and publication]\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas (two binaries with disjoint controller sets). Parallel; potential merge conflicts are a coordination risk, not a dependency. Both build directly on the existing controller-runtime/logr foundation; neither produces an artifact the other builds against. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics — the guideline documents the convention as actually implemented in the manager binary (including the DSCI key migration and cluster-scoped resource handling), so it must be written after the code shape is final. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | Same rule — the published convention must reflect the cloudmanager binary's field usage (shared reconciler, cluster-scoped infrastructure kinds) as built. |\n\nNo Investigation epics were generated (see Open Questions Evaluation below). No onboarding\nepics: rhods-operator is an existing platform component with an architecture-context file\n(`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`), so no\nrepo/license/konflux chain applies.\n\n## Open Questions Evaluation\n\nAll four open questions were evaluated against the decision rule (\"does the answer change\nwhich downstream epics exist or what they do?\"). None qualifies for an Investigation epic:\n\n| Open question | Owner | Verdict | Handling |\n|---------------|-------|---------|----------|\n| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)? | PM / Observability team | Not structural — required field set is fixed by the HLRs; extra fields are additive | Note + open question on the docs epic; guideline documents the required set and may reference optional extensions pending PM decision |\n| Which existing log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns that will change? | SRE / Support | Not structural — identification work inside the regression-validation step | Acceptance criterion on both implementation epics (log consumer regression validation with SRE/Support coordination) |\n| Should cloudmanager ship in the same release as manager or a subsequent one? | PM / ODH Operator team | Not structural — scheduling decision; the cloudmanager epic exists either way | Note on the cloudmanager epic; does not gate or re-shape the DAG |\n| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity? | ODH Operator team | Not structural — the audit is the first phase of the conversion work (already in the effort estimate); the conversion epic does the same work at any percentage | Audit-first scope item + re-split trigger on the manager epic (see Health Warnings) |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n|-----|----------|------|-------|\n| HLR-1 — convert message-embedded name/namespace to structured KV pairs in all reconciliation error logs | P0 | E001, E002 | Split follows the binary boundary: E001 covers manager-binary call sites, E002 covers cloudmanager call sites. Together they cover \"all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs\" (the strategy defines rhods-operator as manager + cloudmanager binaries). |\n| HLR-2 — DSCI controller migrated to standard key names | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller lives in the manager binary. |\n| HLR-3 — service controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | Service controllers live in the manager binary. |\n| HLR-4 — all 15 action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) live in the manager binary. |\n| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | Cloudmanager binary's three controllers. |\n| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include the three fields (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | Module controller and handlers live in the manager binary. |\n| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) — same `log.Error` call sites in the same controller files as the HLR-1 P0 conversion; `resourceKind` is added in the same mechanical pass, and a split would audit and re-touch the same files twice with no independent user value | Not separately schedulable: the P0 conversion rewrites the exact call sites where this P1 field is added. |\n| HLR-8 — document convention as contribution guideline and publish for external adoption | P1 | E003 | Substantive content authoring — distinct work-product type. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Epic Boundary Rationale (within one component/team)\n\n- **Binary boundary (E001 vs E002):** manager and cloudmanager are separate Go binaries\n  with disjoint controller sets and separate deployment targets (OpenShift vs AWS EKS /\n  Azure AKS / CoreWeave) — an architectural sub-system boundary producing independent PR\n  review cycles. The strategy's own effort arithmetic prices cloudmanager separately\n  (~0.25 sprint).\n- **Work-product boundary (E003):** the contribution guideline and published convention\n  are substantive new documentation content, not incidental touch-ups falling out of the\n  code change.\n- **Manager binary kept whole (E001):** the conversion is one mechanical pass over one\n  call-site pattern across controller tiers and action files that share the same\n  convention, the same test approach, and the same lint surface. Splitting per controller\n  tier would fragment a single mechanical change (see Health Warnings for the size\n  management).\n- **CI lint rule is not a standalone epic:** per the shared-surface enforcement rule, each\n  implementation epic authors and verifies the part of the CI lint rule covering its own\n  binary's converted call sites (scope + verification AC on E001 and E002). The lint rule\n  spans exactly the surfaces those epics convert.\n- **COO team (Loki/Tempo operators):** passive recipient of the published convention.\n  Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external\n  operator dependencies, and the RFE approval comment confirms voluntary adoption. No epic\n  is generated for COO; publication (including filing tracking issues in COO projects per\n  the risk mitigation) is scope on the docs epic.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nThe strategy's affected component is **rhods-operator**, which is not a canonical RHAI\nJira component name. All three epics use the canonical component\n**OpenShift AI Productization** — the closest canonical component covering the ODH\nOperator team's rhods-operator work. This mapping is non-obvious and logged here per the\ncomponent-name constraint; `submit.py` would silently strip a non-canonical value.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **E001 size above the ~2-week epic guideline, kept whole with re-split trigger.**\n   E001 is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (~0.5 sprint audit +\n   ~0.75–1 sprint conversion across the manager binary's controller tiers and 15 action\n   files). Kept whole because the conversion is one mechanical pass over the same\n   call-site pattern with a shared convention and shared lint surface — a split would\n   audit the same files twice and fragment one mechanical change. Re-split trigger: the\n   call-site audit confirms a materially higher count than the strategy's 75% embedded-\n   identity estimate (or conversion burn-up exceeds the strategy's estimate), moving the\n   epic past 2 sprints — at that point split along the controller-tier vs action-file\n   boundary (controllers vs the 15 action files), each part remaining a coherent\n   mechanical pass.\n2. **Strategy priority vs HLR labels:** the strategy is priority Major overall while six\n   of eight HLRs are P0 — no inversion, but note HLR-7 (P1) is a queryability enhancement\n   explicitly not a functional requirement; it is bundled into E001 (see traceability\n   row) rather than deferred, because it rides the same call-site pass.\n3. **Process gates pending:** Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation,\n   and Documentation Support are all \"_Pending human review_\" in the strategy's\n   Prerequisites. These are strategy-level process gates, not decomposition unknowns —\n   they do not change which epics exist. The docs epic carries a technical-review AC\n   (Rule 24) and is flagged for the Documentation Support gate.\n4. **Proposed acceptance criteria:** the strategy's AC section is marked \"requires\n   PM/Engineering validation\". The epic ACs below inherit that caveat; the log-consumer\n   regression validation (dev cluster with production log consumer stack, SRE/Support\n   coordination on text-based alert rules) is carried as an AC on both implementation\n   epics per the backwards-compatibility NFR.\n5. **Cloudmanager release timing (open question):** whether cloudmanager lands in the same\n   release as manager is a PM scheduling decision. If deferred, E002 simply schedules\n   later — the DAG needs no change (E003 is blocked by both regardless; if E002 slips a\n   release, E003 should still document the convention from E001's implementation and the\n   publication of the cloudmanager portion follows E002).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable as a split. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team\nadoption of the published convention — but per platform policy that adoption is\nvoluntary, outside RHOAI's control, and explicitly not a deliverable of this strategy.\nAll strategy deliverables (manager conversion, cloudmanager conversion, convention\npublication) are Tier 1 independent work with no upstream-gated Tier 2 component. The\nresidual external risk (adoption delay) is handled as scope on E003 (publish early, file\ntracking issues in COO projects) and as a strategy risk, not as a dependency edge.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":49049,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0b8acca43064110","agent_id":"aff3fafc5d8c33c07","session_id":"c08e1838-c4fd-42e6-9ea2-693cdf6b9521","uuid":"81b8c173-c468-4778-b2b2-38b379aee03f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:51:51.083Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
