{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d82f4067-76fb-421a-8ac6-a10c2ff3f931","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-abfd2e6fadb65ab8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-ejx7_xl5/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Structured resource context in cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic standardizes reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager\nbinary — the companion binary handling multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on AWS EKS,\nAzure AKS, and CoreWeave. Its controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\nCoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation via\nthe shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name`/`namespace` from\ncontroller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts any message-embedded resource\nidentity in their error paths to structured key-value pairs and adds `resourceKind`, so\ncloudmanager logs are queryable by resource identity exactly like the manager binary's.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in the cloudmanager binary's reconciliation error paths\n  (the three infrastructure controllers and any shared reconciler error paths specific to\n  cloudmanager) for message-embedded resource name or namespace.\n- Convert audited call sites that embed resource identity in the message string to\n  structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), keeping `name`/`namespace` flowing\n  from the shared reconciler's request logger where it already does.\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager controller reconciliation\n  error logs (kind is not implied for these controllers in cross-controller queries).\n- Handle the cluster-scoped infrastructure resources (AWSKubernetesEngine,\n  AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine are cluster-scoped CRDs) so logs\n  include `name` and `resourceKind` and omit or empty `namespace` rather than emitting a\n  spurious value.\n- Migrate any non-standard key names found in cloudmanager error paths to the camelCase\n  convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n- Author and verify the portion of the CI lint rule / static-analysis check covering the\n  cloudmanager binary's converted call sites: the check flags any `log.Error` call in\n  cloudmanager scope that embeds name/namespace in message text or omits the required\n  structured fields, and it runs green over the converted code.\n- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for the\n  cloudmanager controllers.\n- Backwards-compatibility validation per the strategy NFR for the cloudmanager log\n  consumers (e.g., CloudWatch on non-OpenShift Kubernetes deployments): before/after log\n  output comparison for a sample reconciliation error and verification that ingestion\n  works without consumer configuration changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged,\n  then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields —\n  measured by unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource\n  name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses\n  structured key-value pairs instead — measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis\n  reporting zero remaining `log.Error` calls in cloudmanager scope with name/namespace\n  embedded in message text.\n- Given a cluster-scoped infrastructure resource is reconciled and encounters an error,\n  when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` and\n  `namespace` is omitted or empty — measured by a unit test confirming no spurious\n  namespace value is emitted.\n- Given the cloudmanager binary's converted call sites, the CI lint rule /\n  static-analysis check covering those sites is authored, wired into CI, and passes over\n  the converted code while demonstrably flagging a non-conforming call in a fixture.\n- Given the log format change, a before/after log output comparison for a sample\n  cloudmanager reconciliation error shows structured fields present and the applicable\n  log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes — measured by the\n  recorded comparison.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P0) — cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs include `name`,\n  `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n- HLR-1 (P0) — cloudmanager-binary portion: conversion of message-embedded name/namespace\n  to structured KV pairs in all rhods-operator (manager + cloudmanager) reconciliation\n  error logs.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Release timing: whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as the manager binary\n  or a subsequent one is an open PM scheduling decision (cloudmanager is only deployed on\n  non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so its impact scope is narrower). This epic's scope and\n  dependencies are unchanged either way; only scheduling moves.\n- These controllers use the shared reconciler and already get `name`/`namespace` from\n  controller-runtime's request logger, so the primary work here is `resourceKind` plus\n  embedded-identity conversion (~0.25 sprint per the strategy estimate).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":54659,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0b8acca43064110","agent_id":"aff3fafc5d8c33c07","session_id":"c08e1838-c4fd-42e6-9ea2-693cdf6b9521","uuid":"b017f3a3-3d1c-41fe-a2e2-f4c2ec69deab","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:52:34.516Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
