{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-1a47957b-93fa-4b62-b64a-3a8103990fab","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8115eea0500c6249","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-ejx7_xl5/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# Structured log field convention: contribution guideline and external publication\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic documents the structured reconciliation-log field convention as a contribution\nguideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator codebase and publishes the\nconvention as a specification for external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator:\nLoki Operator, Tempo Operator) for voluntary adoption per platform policy (overlay 0008 —\nRHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies). The guideline\ncaptures the three-problem diagnosis, the required field set, and the migration guidance\nfor the DSCI key-name change.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline:\n\n- **Contribution guideline (rhods-operator repo)** specifying:\n  - Required structured fields for reconciliation error logs: `name`, `namespace`\n    (omitted or empty for cluster-scoped resources), and `resourceKind`.\n  - camelCase naming rationale — alignment with controller-runtime's existing structured\n    log key conventions.\n  - The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded resource identity, DSCI non-standard\n    key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and missing `resourceKind`.\n  - Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured\n    key-value pairs (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Dashboard 'example' in\n    namespace 'redhat-ods-applications'\")` becoming `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation\n    failed\", \"name\", \"example\", \"namespace\", \"redhat-ods-applications\", \"resourceKind\",\n    \"Dashboard\")`).\n  - Cluster-scoped resource handling (no spurious `namespace` value).\n  - How the CI lint rule / static-analysis check enforces the convention for new code.\n- **DSCI key migration guide** for operators: release-notes-ready documentation of the\n  `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` → `name`/`namespace` migration with before/after\n  Loki/CloudWatch query examples, supporting a single-release transition.\n- **Published convention specification for external operators**: the field convention,\n  rationale, and adoption guidance, published where the COO team (Loki Operator, Tempo\n  Operator) can consume it; file tracking issues in the COO projects per the strategy's\n  risk mitigation, acknowledging adoption is best-effort and at their discretion.\n- Design-doc reconciliation: fold the strategy's \"to be created\" design doc intent\n  (three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, DSCI migration guide) into this\n  guideline rather than producing a separate overlapping document.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The contribution guideline exists in the rhods-operator repository and specifies the\n  required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale,\n  and the three-problem diagnosis — measured by review of the published guideline\n  against this checklist.\n- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded\n  identity and documents cluster-scoped resource handling — measured by the examples\n  being present and consistent with the implemented logging behavior.\n- The DSCI key-name migration is documented with before/after log-query examples\n  suitable for release notes — measured by the migration section covering both the old\n  and new field names.\n- The convention is published as a specification accessible to the external operator\n  teams, and tracking issues are filed in the COO projects — measured by the publication\n  location and the filed issues.\n- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented logging changes in both\n  operator binaries (manager and cloudmanager) — measured by a recorded technical review\n  confirming the documented convention matches the shipped log output, including field\n  names and cluster-scoped behavior.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-8 (P1) — document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller\n  development and publish it for external operator adoption.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Depends on the implementation of the structured logging conversion in both operator\n  binaries being final: the guideline documents what was actually built, including the\n  DSCI migration and cluster-scoped resource behavior.\n- Open question (PM / Observability team): whether the convention should include fields\n  beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category). The\n  required field set is fixed by this strategy; any extension is an additive PM decision\n  that can be layered onto the guideline later and does not gate this epic.\n- External adoption by the COO team is voluntary and outside this strategy's control;\n  the deliverable here is the published convention and the filed tracking issues, not\n  adoption itself.\n- Documentation Support is listed as \"_Pending human review_\" in the strategy's\n  prerequisites; this epic should be routed through that gate when scheduled.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":55907,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0b8acca43064110","agent_id":"aff3fafc5d8c33c07","session_id":"c08e1838-c4fd-42e6-9ea2-693cdf6b9521","uuid":"fc6f3604-5d1e-4430-8bf6-5d1e7715dadd","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:52:50.911Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
