{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab95a99fa06c87dd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Structured resource context in cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: null\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: -1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 6\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Structured resource context in cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic standardizes reconciliation error logs in the rhods-operator cloudmanager\n34\tbinary — the companion binary handling multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on AWS EKS,\n35\tAzure AKS, and CoreWeave. Its controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n36\tCoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation via\n37\tthe shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name`/`namespace` from\n38\tcontroller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts any message-embedded resource\n39\tidentity in their error paths to structured key-value pairs and adds `resourceKind`, so\n40\tcloudmanager logs are queryable by resource identity exactly like the manager binary's.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in the cloudmanager binary's reconciliation error paths\n45\t  (the three infrastructure controllers and any shared reconciler error paths specific to\n46\t  cloudmanager) for message-embedded resource name or namespace.\n47\t- Convert audited call sites that embed resource identity in the message string to\n48\t  structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), keeping `name`/`namespace` flowing\n49\t  from the shared reconciler's request logger where it already does.\n50\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager controller reconciliation\n51\t  error logs (kind is not implied for these controllers in cross-controller queries).\n52\t- Handle the cluster-scoped infrastructure resources (AWSKubernetesEngine,\n53\t  AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine are cluster-scoped CRDs) so logs\n54\t  include `name` and `resourceKind` and omit or empty `namespace` rather than emitting a\n55\t  spurious value.\n56\t- Migrate any non-standard key names found in cloudmanager error paths to the camelCase\n57\t  convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n58\t- Author and verify the portion of the CI lint rule / static-analysis check covering the\n59\t  cloudmanager binary's converted call sites: the check flags any `log.Error` call in\n60\t  cloudmanager scope that embeds name/namespace in message text or omits the required\n61\t  structured fields, and it runs green over the converted code.\n62\t- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for the\n63\t  cloudmanager controllers.\n64\t- Backwards-compatibility validation per the strategy NFR for the cloudmanager log\n65\t  consumers (e.g., CloudWatch on non-OpenShift Kubernetes deployments): before/after log\n66\t  output comparison for a sample reconciliation error and verification that ingestion\n67\t  works without consumer configuration changes.\n68\t\n69\t## Acceptance Criteria\n70\t\n71\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine,\n72\t  CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged,\n73\t  then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields —\n74\t  measured by unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n75\t- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource\n76\t  name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses\n77\t  structured key-value pairs instead — measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis\n78\t  reporting zero remaining `log.Error` calls in cloudmanager scope with name/namespace\n79\t  embedded in message text.\n80\t- Given a cluster-scoped infrastructure resource is reconciled and encounters an error,\n81\t  when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` and\n82\t  `namespace` is omitted or empty — measured by a unit test confirming no spurious\n83\t  namespace value is emitted.\n84\t- Given the cloudmanager binary's converted call sites, the CI lint rule /\n85\t  static-analysis check covering those sites is authored, wired into CI, and passes over\n86\t  the converted code while demonstrably flagging a non-conforming call in a fixture.\n87\t- Given the log format change, a before/after log output comparison for a sample\n88\t  cloudmanager reconciliation error shows structured fields present and the applicable\n89\t  log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes — measured by the\n90\t  recorded comparison.\n91\t\n92\t## HLR Traceability\n93\t\n94\t- HLR-5 (P0) — cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs include `name`,\n95\t  `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n96\t- HLR-1 (P0) — cloudmanager-binary portion: conversion of message-embedded name/namespace\n97\t  to structured KV pairs in all rhods-operator (manager + cloudmanager) reconciliation\n98\t  error logs.\n99\t\n100\t## Notes\n101\t\n102\t- Release timing: whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as the manager binary\n103\t  or a subsequent one is an open PM scheduling decision (cloudmanager is only deployed on\n104\t  non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so its impact scope is narrower). This epic's scope and\n105\t  dependencies are unchanged either way; only scheduling moves.\n106\t- These controllers use the shared reconciler and already get `name`/`namespace` from\n107\t  controller-runtime's request logger, so the primary work here is `resourceKind` plus\n108\t  embedded-identity conversion (~0.25 sprint per the strategy estimate).\n109\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-baca80cab9da882e","agent_id":"a9df7a535a36364ba","session_id":"c08e1838-c4fd-42e6-9ea2-693cdf6b9521","uuid":"8c8c2cb7-d035-417f-82ae-1f7e2819104d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:54:12.501Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab95a99fa06c87dd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
